.SS Abstract
This header provides a \f[B]minimal abstraction for register\-based
argument passing\f[R] on \f[B]32\-bit x86 systems\f[R], using the
compiler\-specific \f[CR]fastcall\f[R] calling convention where
supported.
.PP
On classic \f[B]32\-bit x86 (i386)\f[R] systems, the default C calling
convention (\f[CR]cdecl\f[R]) passes \f[B]all function arguments on the
stack\f[R].
Some compilers (notably \f[B]MSVC and GCC\-compatible\f[R]) provide
extensions that allow \f[B]a limited number of arguments to be passed
via CPU registers\f[R], reducing call overhead for small, frequently
called functions.
.PP
The \f[CR]FASTCALL\f[R] macro annotates a function (or function pointer)
to request that arguments be passed using the compiler\(aqs
\f[B]fastcall\f[R] convention, when supported by the compiler and target
architecture.
.PP
When unsupported, \f[CR]FASTCALL\f[R] safely degrades to a no\-op,
preserving source compatibility without altering behavior.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Important\f[R]: \f[CR]fastcall\f[R] is \f[B]not a standardized
ABI\f[R].
It is compiler\-specific and must be used consistently across
declarations, definitions, and function pointers.
.RE
